WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the things you've
talked about on your platform

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:03.203 line:92%
that I want to ask you about

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:05.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the idea of raising
teachers' salaries.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.074
Because a lot of people have
different ideas on education.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-And I feel like you define

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.344
yourself because you're not
saying, "Let's not put more

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
money in schools;
let's put more money
in teachers' pockets."

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:16.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
Explain how you came
to that decision.

00:00:16.216 --> 00:00:18.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, first of all,
personal story.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:20.887 size:84% position:42%
My first-grade teacher,
Mrs. Francis Wilson,

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.088 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
God rest her soul,

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:23.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
attended my law-school
graduation.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:24.491 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-That's fantastic.

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:27.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think most of us would say
that there was a teacher

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
or more than one teacher
in our lives

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
who convinced us
that we were special.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:34.868 line:92%
We weren't particularly special,

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:37.103 size:81% position:41%
but they convinced us,
and we believed them.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:40.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's part of what led us
to be in these situations

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.942 size:81% position:41%
where we have achieved
any level of success.

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:45.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are two groups of people
raising our children --

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:48.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
Parents, and it
could be grandparents,
aunties, and uncles...

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:48.982 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and teachers.

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we are not paying
our teachers their value.

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:54.220 line:92%
Teachers are paid 10% or more

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
less than similarly educated
college graduates.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:01:00.193
And so what I am proposing is,
the first time in our nation,

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:02.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
what would be
a federal investment

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:03.263 line:92%
in closing the teacher pay gap,

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:07.200
which would result in closing
a gap of about $13,500 a year.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.138
$13,500 a year means a year's
worth of mortgage payments

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.773 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
in most places.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:14.374 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
$13,500 a year

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:17.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
means a year's worth
of groceries in most places.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.511 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
$13,500 a year

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:21.381
means putting a significant dent
in student loan debt.

00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:25.251
And the way I think about, Seth,
is that we've got students

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.488
who are in college right now
who are excelling in science

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:31.191
who would love nothing more than
to teach middle-school students.

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:32.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
But they're looking
at student loan debt,

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:35.195
they're looking at their bills,
and they've decided,

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:37.163 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Maybe I need to go
and work on Wall Street

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I need to
pay these things off."

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:40.967 align:start size:94% position:6%
I want that person
teaching in that school.

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:44.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, frankly, I think
that you can judge a society

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:47.807 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on the way
it treats its children.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:50.477 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:50.477 --> 00:01:51.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
Right?
-Yeah.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:55.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And one of the greatest
expressions of love

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:59.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
that a society
can give to its children

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:01.254 line:92%
is to invest in their education.

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:04.057
And by extension,
that means investing in teachers

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:05.024 line:92%
who are right now working

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
two and three jobs
to pay the bills.

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:08.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, as the son
of a public-school teacher,

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:11.331
I would like you to know that
she would have blown that money

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:13.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
as quickly
as you gave it to her.

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:14.567 size:75% position:38% line:92%
She's a unique case.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think most people
would be better with it.

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
But she --
-No, but seriously --

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:20.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, seriously,
I have met teachers --

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:22.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
First of all,
94% of the teachers

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
in our schools right now,
in public schools --

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:28.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
94% of them are coming
out of their own pocket
to pay for school supplies.

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:31.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have talked to teachers,
one in South Carolina

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:34.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was busing tables
as her second job

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then realized
in the same restaurant

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:38.758
there were four other teachers
that were also busing tables,

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they have
a passion to teach,

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:41.561 line:92%
but they got to pay the bills.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
I want them,
instead of a second job,

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:45.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
to spend that time
in professional development

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
and all the things
they want to do.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.269 line:92%
And so that's why I'm proposing

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:51.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
the first-ever investment
in teacher pay.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.640 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, that's wonderful.
I'm very happy to hear that.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:59.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
I want to ask --
You have a longer --

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:01.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
Certainly a longer record
in law enforcement

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.550 line:92%
than you do as a senator.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:04.884 line:92%
It was 2002? When did you start?

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I was elected in 2003.
-2003.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:11.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
Some progressives
have taken issue with the way

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:15.461
you were a law-enforcement
official over those years.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:20.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel as though
your perspective on things

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
has evolved since
you first started the job?

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:24.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or do you feel as though
your record is sort of being

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
unfairly looked at
by progressives

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.242
who think that maybe there was
a problem with overincarceration

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:31.010 size:81% position:41%
or issues like that?
-Well, let's be clear.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
America has a problem
with mass incarceration.

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:35.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's be clear.
The criminal justice system

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:38.451
of our country is deeply flawed
and needs to be fixed

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:41.621 size:84% position:42%
and has, in many cases,
been informed by bias

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:44.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
and informed by
I think a very --

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:45.558 line:92%
frankly, backward policy,

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:48.328
which is that it has been formed
in a way that is about reacting

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:50.930
to crime after it happens
instead of thinking about

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
preventing crime
before it happens.

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:55.902
I made a very conscious decision
to become a prosecutor.

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:58.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
My parents were active
in the Civil Rights Movement.

00:03:58.004 --> 00:04:00.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
My sister went on
to lead the ACLU.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.408 align:start size:94% position:6%
When I decided
to be a prosecutor,

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:05.445 size:78% position:39%
it was because I felt
that this is a system

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:06.279 line:92%
that needs to be reformed

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:10.049 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I want to have a job
of doing it from the inside.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:12.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's why
I was able to create,

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.153 line:92%
one of the first in the nation,

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:17.357
focuses of a D.A.'S office
on re-entry initiatives.

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:18.424 size:84% position:42% line:92%
I created an initiative

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:19.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
that ended up being
a national model

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:23.496 align:start size:94% position:6%
around bringing
drug-sales offenders,

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:27.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
getting them into
job counseling and jobs

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:27.634 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and, in that way,

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:30.069
reducing the likelihood
that they would reoffend.

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:32.138 line:92%
And I'm proud of that work.

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:34.107 line:92%
But, you know, I'm also thankful

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:37.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
that over the intervening
15 years

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:38.511 line:92%
that our country has evolved

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:41.547
to understand we really
have got to reform this system.

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:44.984
The tough-on-crime approach
to criminal-justice policy

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:48.821
is not smart, and we have
got to take a position

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:51.958
that instead of saying soft
on crime or tough on crime,

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:54.794
let's be smart on crime,
which means, let's focus on

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:57.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
prevention as an important way
to achieve public safety.

00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:00.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's also recognize
the flaws in the system.

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:02.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I want to ask you
one last question, as well.

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:05.405 size:84% position:42%
I was realizing you won
your election in 2016.

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:06.072 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.841
-That was your first time
serving in the Senate.

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.510 align:start size:94% position:6%
A very exciting night.
-Yes. Yeah.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was also the night
Donald Trump won.

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:14.480 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Was that the weirdest --

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.415 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.415 --> 00:05:16.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that
the weirdest night ever?

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:19.652
Was it like being someone
on the "Titanic" going,

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I've got a boat.
I've got a life raft."

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:24.824 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was really --
First of all, we were going --

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:29.996 align:start size:94% position:6%
We do a friends and family
small dinner before the party.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:31.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were hoping
there would be a party.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:35.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
And my husband kept grunting
as we were in the car

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause he was watching
the ticker tape.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.772
-The needle.
Yeah, we all watched the needle.

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:41.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And he was like --
He was making noises.

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I was like,
"What's going on?"

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.945
And I said, "No, it's too early.
It can't be."

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:49.882
And as the night progressed,
of course, that's what it was.

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:51.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-It was extremely bittersweet.

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:56.689
And I tore up all the notes
that I had prepared to read,

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:58.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I just took the stage
and I said,

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.160 line:92%
"We will fight. We will fight."

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.528 size:84% position:42% line:92%
And then I went home...

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:05.565 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:06.799 size:78% position:39% line:92%
And then I went home,

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:09.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I went home
and I sat on the couch

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:13.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
with a family-sized bag
of nacho Doritos.

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:17.710 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I did not share one chip
with anybody.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I just watched the TV.
Utter shock and dismay.

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:24.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I think
there were very few stores

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:27.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
that had Doritos left
by the following morning.

00:06:27.754 --> 00:06:29.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:32.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
Best of luck
in the ensuing year or so.

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:36.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
Senator Kamala Harris,
everybody.

